WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:04.045
Talk about a multiverse of madness. Sony&nbsp;
Pictures Animation's sequel "Spider-Man:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.045 --> 00:00:07.620
Across the Spider-Verse" not only breaks&nbsp;
new ground in the superhero genre,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:10.828
but it also breaks the understanding&nbsp;
of the Marvel multiverse at large.

00:00:10.828 --> 00:00:15.360
As seemingly established in "Spider-Man: No Way&nbsp;
Home," all Marvel movies — at least those in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:19.740
Marvel Cinematic Universe and those released by&nbsp;
Sony Pictures — co-exist in the same multiverse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:23.040
with every iteration of the titular&nbsp;
web-slinger just a spell away from one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:26.460
another. While "Across the Spider-Verse"&nbsp;
doesn't violate the guidelines set in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:29.700
previous Marvel multiverse romps, it&nbsp;
does build upon them in ways that could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:33.540
affect the MCU's defining multiverse&nbsp;
project — the Disney+ series "Loki."

00:00:33.540 --> 00:00:38.520
"Loki" Season 2 is set to premiere in October&nbsp;
2023, and Season 1 outlined how the multiverse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:43.080
works, grows, and is maintained across all Marvel&nbsp;
properties. Its mind-blowing first-season finale&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:47.280
casts some doubt on the stringency of these&nbsp;
rules, as the mastermind behind the multiversal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.280 --> 00:00:51.780
maintenance turns out to be an unstable Kang&nbsp;
variant known as He Who Remains. Where "Loki"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.780 --> 00:00:54.780
pulled the rug out from audiences in terms&nbsp;
of their understanding of the multiverse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:58.200
"Spider-Verse" introduces new rules&nbsp;
that are seemingly unimpeachable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.200 --> 00:01:01.920
especially in regards to the film's&nbsp;
defining mechanic — canon events.

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:06.540
A canon event is a moment in a Spider-Person's&nbsp;
lifetime that is consistent across all variants&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:09.540
in the Spider-Verse and cannot&nbsp;
be changed without risking total&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:13.190
universal destruction. This includes&nbsp;
tragedies like the death of a loved one.

00:01:13.190 --> 00:01:15.335
"And then I looked at my uncle and…"

00:01:15.335 --> 00:01:18.185
"Uh, let me guess. He died?"

00:01:18.185 --> 00:01:21.840
When this concept is explained at the&nbsp;
Spider-Society headquarters, a hologram&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:25.500
shows an image that looks similar to "Loki's"&nbsp;
branching representation of the multiverse.

00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:29.520
Interestingly enough, an eerily similar&nbsp;
concept is introduced in the Disney+&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:32.888
animated series "What If...?,"&nbsp;
referred to as "absolute points."

00:01:32.888 --> 00:01:37.028
"The power required to break an&nbsp;
absolute point in time is immense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.028 --> 00:01:41.400
and nigh impossible to obtain. Yeah, no doy."

00:01:41.400 --> 00:01:45.480
The first absolute point delineated as&nbsp;
such is the death of Christine Palmer in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:48.955
the episode "What if... Doctor Strange&nbsp;
Lost His Heart Instead of His Hands?"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.955 --> 00:01:52.360
Over the course of the story, viewers&nbsp;
see that absolute points are nearly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.360 --> 00:01:55.503
impossible to change without some&nbsp;
sort of multiversal intervention.

00:01:55.503 --> 00:02:00.180
When Doctor Strange Supreme manages to do just&nbsp;
that, his world begins to dissolve into blackness.

00:02:00.180 --> 00:02:04.200
In "Across the Spider-Verse," Miles&nbsp;
Morales inadvertently shows fans what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:07.140
a decaying universe looks like when he&nbsp;
saves the life of the police captain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:11.340
of Pavitr Prabhakar's Earth-50101.&nbsp;
The eerie, all-consuming blackness&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.340 --> 00:02:14.940
bears a resemblance to similar events in&nbsp;
"What If...?" and "Doctor Strange in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.940 --> 00:02:19.260
Multiverse of Madness." This could mean that&nbsp;
in every universe, what audiences see is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:24.060
inter-universal apocalypse known as an incursion.&nbsp;
What is an incursion? Take it away, Reed.

00:02:24.060 --> 00:02:29.880
"An incursion occurs when the boundary&nbsp;
between universes corrodes and they collide."

00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:33.300
By violating the boundaries between&nbsp;
universes, Miles and Doctor Strange&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:37.200
unintentionally altered canon events,&nbsp;
or absolute points if you will. Though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:40.860
this newfound specificity is small, it&nbsp;
creates guidelines that, going forward,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:45.603
could change how heroes and villains deal with&nbsp;
the Marvel Universe's newest overarching threat.

00:02:45.603 --> 00:02:47.580
"Spider-Verse" also presents a key that could save&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:50.510
the fate of the entire Marvel&nbsp;
multiverse in the near future.

00:02:50.510 --> 00:02:54.478
"You say the fate of the&nbsp;
multiverse and my brain dies."

00:02:54.478 --> 00:02:57.420
Until "Across the Spider-Verse," there&nbsp;
were only a handful of beings capable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.420 --> 00:03:01.260
of traversing the multiverse intentionally.&nbsp;
Now, there is a possibly infinite number of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:05.520
Spider-People that possess the ability to travel&nbsp;
the multiverse and seemingly prevent incursions.

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:10.380
As seen on Earth-50101, Miguel O'Hara and his team&nbsp;
are able to keep the blackness at bay by arriving&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:15.060
on the scene immediately. This is a skill that&nbsp;
neither Doctor Strange nor "Loki's" TVA have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:19.500
able to pull off. As the second season of "Loki"&nbsp;
continues to explore and elaborate upon how the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.500 --> 00:03:23.700
multiverse works, it will likely come up against&nbsp;
the subject of incursions and how they are caused.

00:03:23.938 --> 00:03:27.778
That could lay the groundwork for Loki to&nbsp;
discover a way to prevent incursions himself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.778 --> 00:03:32.400
thus staving off the inevitably messy aftermath of&nbsp;
Kang's meddling in the multiverse. It would be a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:36.780
fitting end to the character's story, as he learns&nbsp;
how to maintain order without the quasi-murderous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.780 --> 00:03:41.940
ideologies of Miguel O'Hara and He Who Remains.&nbsp;
Instead of pruning, Loki could stimulate growth.

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:47.100
"Big metaphor guy. I like it.&nbsp;
Makes you sound super smart."

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:51.660
Narratively, a focus on growth would be the&nbsp;
polar opposite of The Infinity Saga's ethos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.660 --> 00:03:55.800
which communicated that victory demands&nbsp;
sacrifice. As skillfully as this message&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.800 --> 00:03:58.860
is conveyed, it would be fitting for&nbsp;
the new era of the Marvel multiverse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.860 --> 00:04:01.740
to usher in something different by&nbsp;
embracing the ability for comic book&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.740 --> 00:04:05.880
stories to evolve beyond reader's wildest&nbsp;
imaginations, without all the nihilism.

00:04:05.880 --> 00:04:09.000
The perfect place to set up this&nbsp;
much-needed shift would be in films&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:12.480
like "Across the Spider-Verse" and TV&nbsp;
series like "Loki," which tell stories&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:16.080
about the wonders of expanding worlds and&nbsp;
the potential beauty of creative evolution.

